820 0 obj
<<
/Title (Log Read Behavior The system usesa d irect file-read approach. The backend reads threat_intel.json on every request,  including dashboard loads and /api/threats polling \(every ~5 seconds\). This ensures real-time accuracy and simplicity, with no cache synchronization overhead. For small datasets \(<10,000 logs\), disk I/O and JSON parsing remain efficient. However, performance may degrade as data volume increases due to repeated reads and parsing.)
/Dest [18 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 631 0 R
/Next 917 0 R
>>
endobj
821 0 obj
<<
/Title (Limitations)
/Dest [18 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 631 0 R
/Prev 918 0 R
/First 919 0 R
/Last 920 0 R
/Count -4
>>
endobj
822 0 obj
<<
/Title (Honeypot Isolation: The service runs on a dedicated port \(8888\) with no access to real production data, healthcare records, or system resources. No Payload Execution: The socket listener only reads the first 1024 bytes \(optional\) and  never executes any received data. Deception Integrity: The deceptive message does not reveal the true nature of the system  \(no "honeypot" text, only realistic healthcare error messages\). Log Integrity: JSON threat logs are append-only; no modification or deletion capability is  exposed via the network. Dashboard Exposure: The dashboard \(port 5001\) binds to localhost only by default. In  production healthcare environments, it must be behind a reverse proxy with authentication.)
/Dest [19 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 632 0 R
>>
endobj
823 0 obj
<<
/Title (Concurrent Connections: Support at least 50 simultaneous TCP connections using Python threads \(simulating DDoS or scanning\). Log Write Latency: < 10 ms per threat log entry \(excluding disk I/O\). Dashboard Response Time: < 200 ms for /api/threats with up to 10,000 log entries. Resource Limits: CPU usage < 5% idle, Memory < 50 MB under normal attack load. Uptime: Designed to run continuously for 30+ days without restart in healthcare SOC environments.)
/Dest [19 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 633 0 R
>>
endobj
